  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe main story over the next 4 to 5 days will be the mild \ntemperatures by mid-November standards. Normal highs/lows for \nGreen Bay this week are 43/29 degrees. Forecast high temps through\nSaturday will be in the 50s, with the probability of exceeding 60\ndegrees near 70-80% across the Fox Valley both Thursday and \nSaturday. Overnight lows will also be mild, ranging anywhere from \nthe mid 30s to upper 40s. Some high min temp records could break \non Saturday morning. This warmth is being brought up by \nstrengthening southwest flow, which also means gusty conditions \nreaching the surface at times, particularly during the afternoons \nwith gusts of 20 to 30 mph. \n\nThe chance for rain on Thursday and Friday continues to decrease \nas the surface system tracks farther norther. Probabilistic \nguidance only shows a 10-15% chance of 0.01\" of rain in central WI\nduring this time frame, and near zero elsewhere. \n\nThe greater opportunity for precip is Friday night through Sunday as\na system tracks from the central Plains to the western Great \nLakes. Right now, the most likely path of this low puts the Green \nBay forecast area in the warm sector, pulling in amble Gulf \nmoisture ahead of it as pwats surge to around 1-inch (which is \nright around the climatological max for late November). NBM \nprobabilities show a 30-60% probability for at least 0.25\" of rain\nby 6AM Sunday, highest across central and north-central WI. As \nthe system departs, cooler air wrapping around the back of the \nlow could result in some light snow for north-central WI on \nSunday. Probabilities for 0.1\" of snow Sunday-Sunday night are \n20-50% in north-central WI, and drop to around 10% for 1\" of snow.\nOf course, all these details will come down to the exact path the\nlow takes which will be monitored over the next few days.",
